sk|E|
pc|C9|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Let's count where East is going to get the tricks. If spades split 3-3, then East gets 5 spades, 2 clubs, and 2 diamonds for 9 tricks for sure. But if they don't split then there could be a problem as it is unlikely that diamonds will run without losing a trick. |
PG| ***** 21 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|However, as it turns out, there is a lucky distribution in diamonds. So another possibility is to play for 3-2 diamonds or a singleton @DQ or @D10, play @DA, @DK, and another diamond. |
PG| ***** 22 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|On the other hand, North/South might be able to take 4 hearts and beat the hand then if we find that defense. But that will be far from obvious.|
PG| ***** 23 ***** |


