UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Luckily, Declarer got a very lucky diamond split.|
PG| ***** 37 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|North did not watch my signals. North should 100% know to keep the @CK for several reasons. First of all I led count in clubs. So North knows East started with 3--because of the club 9 lead. Second, I signaled in hearts so he knows I have the @HK.|
PG| ***** 38 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|And third of all, as a good defender and partner, if I had the 3rd club I would have discarded it when East was running diamonds to give partner a count in clubs. As I often say, defense is very hard as you can see, the defense did not take all of their tricks on either of the first two hands. |
PG| ***** 39 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|That is one reason why aggressive bidding pays off. No one, not even the best players in the world, plays perfect defense. With 8 diamonds, leading the @DA, @DK, and low diamond works any time diamonds are 3-2 or 4-1 with a singleton Queen or 10.|
PG| ***** 40 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|As one of Kibs questioned leading a low diamond will work when diamonds are 3-2. But if diamonds are 4-1 with a singleton @DQ that will give away a trick. |
PG| ***** 41 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|On this particular suit combination playing @DAK first is best. This points out the difficulty of figuring out what is called suit combinations. You have to be somewhat mathematical to figure out how to play uncommon combinations. Fortunately, there are many common suit combinations which you end up learning. However, the uncommon ones are harder to figure out.

|
PG| ***** 42 ***** |


