mb|P|mb|4C|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I agree with 4@C though, you could make a case for opening 1@C with 10 HCP and 10 cards in your two longest suits.|
PG| ***** 80 ***** |


MB|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|North has a pretty clear 4@S call over 4@C.|
PG| ***** 81 ***** |


mb|4S|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|MB|?|

cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|5@C has to be a good bid. Whenever partner preempts and with a likely 11 card fit, it is almost always best if you play in their suit.|
PG| ***** 82 ***** |


mb|5C|MB|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now I have a problem what to do. I know we have a likely 10 card fit in spades. But I have a very bad hand and 5@S could be down several despite my singleton club. There is a general principle which many find hard to follow.|
PG| ***** 83 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|That is ^*BThe 5 Level Belongs to the Opponents^*n meaning it is rarely right to bid 5 over 5. If I had a club void I would bid 5@S as that is one exception. But I will follow the principle and pass. Further, it is possible they are going down.|
PG| ***** 84 ***** |


mb|P|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P|
pg| ***** 85 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The singleton heart is best lead. A club is 2nd choice. Leading unprotected aces is usually a bad idea. Further as you can see leading either ace gives away a trick.|
PG| ***** 86 ***** |


